Alex, I couldn't agree more with your instincts here.

You know, after our Barcelona conversation, I had a feeling you'd gravitate toward Le Petit Comptoir. And honestly? It's the right call.

Café de la Tour is beautiful - I won't pretend otherwise. That Eiffel Tower view is genuinely spectacular. But here's the thing: locals don't eat there. The popularity among Parisians is low, and while the food is competent, you're essentially paying sixty-five euros for a view and a photo opportunity. The quality just doesn't match what you're looking for.

Le Petit Comptoir is the real Paris. Chef Michel Durand represents something that's becoming increasingly rare - a true neighborhood bistro where the cooking hasn't been adjusted for international palates or Instagram aesthetics. When you sit at one of those worn wooden tables and see the handwritten menu, you're experiencing the same thing Parisians in that neighborhood have experienced for generations. The boeuf bourguignon, the duck confit, the tarte Tatin - these are dishes made with care and tradition, not performance.

A few practical notes: They're closed Sundays and Mondays, and Tuesday through Thursday they only serve lunch. Friday and Saturday they're open for dinner until ten. I'd recommend calling ahead - the number is plus thirty-three one forty-three seventy-nine twelve forty-seven - since it's a small space and regulars often have their usual tables. A few words of French will go a long way, even just the basics.

I recommend Le Petit Comptoir.

You're going to love it, Alex. Bon appétit!

