## **Option 1: Can Paixano (La Xampanyeria)**  
📍 Carrer de la Reina Cristina 7, Barceloneta

**Why locals go:**  
This is a Barcelona *institution*. Standing-room-only cava bar where locals have been cramming in since forever for dirt-cheap sparkling wine (€1.50/glass for house cava — the pink is iconic) and enormous bocadillos. It's chaotic, loud, zero pretense. You'll be shoulder-to-shoulder with dock workers, office crowds, and neighborhood regulars. No tourists who can't handle the vibe survive here.

**What to order:**  
- **House cava** (pink sparkler) — non-negotiable, order multiple rounds
- **Bocadillo mixto**: jamón ibérico + manchego + fuet. Enormous, messy, perfect.
- **Jamón carved to order** if you want a plate to share
- Keep it simple: cured meats, cheese, bread, cava. That's the whole point.

**What to avoid:**  
Don't overthink it. There's no "menu" really — just sandwiches and cava. The only mistake is expecting table service or trying to linger for 2 hours. This isn't that kind of place.

**Reservation policy + strategy:**  
- **No reservations. Cash only.**
- **Best move**: Arrive **19:00–19:30 on Tuesday or Wednesday**. Peak chaos is 19:30–21:00 (post-work crowd), so if you get there right as it's building, you'll snag space at the bar.
- Thursday gets busier. Avoid Friday/Saturday unless you love sardine-can vibes.
- Closed Sundays.
- You'll spend 30–45 min max. It's a quick hit, not a long dinner.

---

## **Option 2: La Rambla Paella House**  
📍 La Rambla 78, Ciutat Vella

**Why locals go:**  
Okay, hear me out — I know La Rambla has a reputation, but this spot has managed to stay authentic despite the location. Locals (especially families and groups celebrating something) come here for **proper Valencian paella** cooked in massive traditional pans. The terrace has that scenic Rambla energy, but the *real* magic is the kitchen: they make paella fresh to order, which most tourist traps don't bother with. You'll see Spanish-speaking tables who've been coming for years, especially on weekends.

The flamenco nights (Thu–Sat) also draw neighborhood crowds, not just tourists.

**What to order:**  
- **Seafood paella** (their signature — get the full portion for 2, takes ~25min to prepare fresh)
- **Sangria pitcher** (comes complimentary with set menu, or order it anyway)
- **Crema catalana** for dessert
- Start with a **tapas platter** if you're hungry while paella cooks

**What to avoid:**  
- Skip the à la carte pricing — **go for the set menu**, way better value  
- Don't sit on the terrace if you want to avoid hawkers (they occasionally approach outdoor tables). Ask for indoor or the semi-private room if you want less Rambla chaos.

**Reservation policy + strategy:**  
- **Reservations recommended but walk-ins accepted.**
- Book online (https://www.laramblapaella.com/reserve) for **21:00 on Wednesday** — that's late enough that the early tourist wave has cleared, but you'll still catch local diners.
- If you walk in, arrive by **20:15–20:30** to avoid waits.
- They're open late (til midnight weekdays, 1am Fri/Sat), so no rush.

---

## **My take:**

If you want **maximum local chaos and budget-friendly**, do **Can Paixano** early in the evening (19:00ish Tuesday), then grab dinner elsewhere after.

If you want a **full sit-down dinner with authentic paella and a bit more comfort**, go **La Rambla Paella House** at 21:00 Wednesday.

Both are legit. Can Paixano is the scrappier, more "if you know you know" vibe. La Rambla is the rare La Rambla spot that hasn't sold out.

**Which direction are you leaning?**

