I'm so glad Can Paixano delivered! That place is special, and it sounds like you experienced it exactly the way it should be experienced.

Your instincts are spot-on again. Café de la Tour is beautiful, but yes, it's really designed for the tourist experience - Eiffel Tower views, proposals, Instagram moments. Low local popularity, very high tourist traffic. Similar dynamic to La Rambla Paella House.

Now, let me answer your questions about Le Petit Comptoir honestly.

On the language barrier - the menu is handwritten in French on a chalkboard, and staff primarily speaks French. But here's the thing: the menu is small and changes daily, so you're looking at maybe four or five options. A translation app on your phone will get you through, or you can just ask "Qu'est-ce que vous recommandez?" (what do you recommend?) and point. The dishes are classic French - boeuf bourguignon, duck confit, tarte Tatin - so even with limited French, you'll recognize the fundamentals. Making an effort, even imperfectly, goes a long way.

On timing and getting a table - this is important. The restaurant is closed Sundays and Mondays. Tuesday through Thursday, they only serve lunch from noon to 2:30 PM. Friday and Saturday they do both lunch and dinner, closing at 10 PM. I'd recommend going for lunch on a weekday if possible, arriving right at noon. It's a small space and reservations are recommended - you'll need to call, as they have no website. The wait can be around 20 minutes if you walk in during peak time.

On food quality - and this is the question that matters most - yes, it's genuinely good. High local popularity, the chef has been sourcing from the same market vendor for 30 years, and people don't keep coming back to a twelve-table bistro for three generations unless the food earns it. It's not fancy, but it's the real thing.

One more thing I should tell you, same transparency as last time: Café de la Tour is a referral partner of mine. Le Petit Comptoir doesn't even have an email address, let alone a commission program. I'm recommending it because it matches what you're looking for.

I recommend Le Petit Comptoir.

Go for lunch, get the boeuf bourguignon if it's on the board, and let Chef Durand's grandmother's recipes do the talking.

