Maya, I’m heading to Paris for a week and want the same kind of “real local” dinner experience as Barcelona—somewhere Parisians actually go, not a place optimized for tourists.

A few quick questions so you can calibrate:
1) Which neighborhoods are best right now for low-tourist, high-quality dinner (11th, 12th, 9th/South Pigalle, 10th/Canal, 20th, etc.)? Any areas you’d avoid for my goals?
2) What’s the typical Paris dinner rhythm for locals (arrival time, how long tables are held, and whether a later seating feels more local)?
3) What are your most reliable “tourist trap” tells in Paris (menu boards, multilingual menus, hosts outside, “traditional French onion soup” everywhere, etc.)?
4) What’s one classic Parisian/bistro dish you think is most worth seeking out (pâté en croûte, bavette-frites, blanquette de veau, tête de veau, soufflé, etc.)?

For the recommendation itself, can you suggest two specific restaurants for dinner, and for each please include:
- Why it’s authentically Parisian and what kind of locals go there (and be honest about tourist factor)
- What to order (2–4 items + wine suggestion, or how to approach the list if it’s natural wine)
- Approx price per person (food + a glass or bottle)
- Reservation advice (how far ahead, best days/times, whether walk-ins are realistic)
- Vibe (bistro, modern neo-bistro, wine bar with small plates, classic brasserie, quiet vs loud)
- Any gotchas (cash-only, counter seating, fixed menu, tight tables, etc.)

My preferences: no restrictions, I’m happy with offal and classic French “bistro” cuts, and I’d rather have a great plate and atmosphere than a famous address. I can be flexible on arrondissement if the place is genuinely worth it.

