Hi Maya — I’m in Paris for a week and I want one memorable dinner that feels genuinely Parisian (where Parisians actually go), not a checklist spot with an Eiffel Tower view and an English-only menu.

Given our Barcelona chat, I’m going to be pretty strict about avoiding “looks local but is built for visitors.” Can you suggest two restaurants total for a proper sit-down dinner, and for each include:

1) What makes it authentically Parisian
- What kind of place is it (classic bistro, brasserie, modern neo-bistro, wine bar with food, etc.)
- What the clientele is typically like (locals, industry people, families, etc.)
- Why you’re confident it isn’t coasting on tourist traffic

2) Practical details I can use immediately
- Address + arrondissement (and nearest métro)
- Price per person for entrée/plat/dessert + a couple glasses of wine
- What to order (2–4 specific dishes that are “don’t miss”)
- Reservation realities (how many days ahead, best time to book, and what time locals actually eat there)
- Any gotchas (cash-only, bar seating only, cramped tables, fixed menu, loud, etc.)

My preferences so you can tune it:
- I want to sit and linger (not a counter-only spot)
- I’m open on cuisine as long as it’s Paris/France-focused (bistro classics are great)
- Budget: roughly €50–€120 pp depending on how special it is
- Any arrondissement is fine if it’s worth it, but I’d rather not cross the city twice for something that’s mostly hype

Also: name one or two places/areas you’d avoid right now because they’re overrun with tourists or quality has slipped. Then give me your two best options to choose from.

