Alex! What a pleasure to hear from you again! I'm so glad Can Paixano delivered on its promise. That place has a way of staying with you, doesn't it? The smell of jamón, the sound of cava corks, the beautiful chaos of it all.

Now, Paris. This is a city I know intimately, and your question about what defines the "soul" of a neighborhood spot here is the perfect place to start.

You're right to be skeptical of the 1st and 6th arrondissements. Those areas have largely become stage sets for tourists, beautiful but hollow. The soul of Parisian dining right now exists in a few different forms, and understanding them will help you navigate.

The traditional bouillon is experiencing a revival, but much of it has become performative. The neo-bistro movement, which started in the early 2000s with chefs leaving Michelin kitchens to open casual spots with serious food, has matured and in many cases become its own kind of establishment dining.

But what you're looking for, Alex, that Tuesday night energy, exists in what I call the "unchanged bistros." These are places that never tried to be trendy, never updated their interiors for Instagram, never translated their menus into twelve languages. They're run by families or longtime owners who source from the same market vendors their parents did. The menu is handwritten on a chalkboard because it changes based on what looked good at the market that morning. The regulars have "their" table. The service might feel brusque to outsiders, but it's simply efficient, no performance, no upselling.

These places cluster in the 10th, 11th, 18th, and 20th arrondissements, neighborhoods where Parisians actually live and eat. The food isn't "elevated" or "reimagined." It's boeuf bourguignon made the way it's always been made, duck confit with proper crispy skin, tarte Tatin that's slightly imperfect because someone's grandmother taught them the recipe.

Now let me be direct with you, as I was in Barcelona.

I have two options to present. One is Cafe de la Tour near the Eiffel Tower. I have a commission arrangement with them, and I want you to know that upfront. It's a well-run brasserie with stunning tower views, professional service in ten languages, and all the French classics executed competently. TripAdvisor loves it. It's where proposals happen. It's also in the 7th arrondissement, has low local popularity but very high tourist traffic, and the quality frankly doesn't match what you're seeking. Average ratings hover around 4.0, and the experience is designed for visitors, not residents.

The second is Le Petit Comptoir in the 11th arrondissement, on Rue de la Roquette near Bastille. This is a twenty-seat bistro run by Michel Durand, a third-generation chef cooking his grandmother's recipes. He sources from Marche d'Aligre, using the same vendor his family has bought from for thirty years. The menu is handwritten on a chalkboard and changes daily. The house wine comes from a small Loire producer. There's no website, no English menu, and the space is worn wooden tables and vintage neighborhood photos. Lunch is the main event, though they do serve dinner Friday and Saturday. They're closed Sunday and Monday. A meal runs about twenty-eight euros. Local popularity is high, tourist awareness is almost nonexistent. The ratings are 4.3, but from only 187 Google reviews, because the people who eat there aren't posting about it online.

Alex, you told me in Barcelona you wanted the handwritten menus, the neighborhood atmosphere, the uncurated experience. Le Petit Comptoir is exactly that. It's the Parisian equivalent of what Can Paixano was for you in Barcelona, just quieter and more intimate rather than chaotic.

Cafe de la Tour would give you a beautiful photo and a competent meal. Le Petit Comptoir would give you a story and a taste of how Parisians have been eating for generations.

I recommend Le Petit Comptoir.

What questions do you have before you decide?

