Alex! So great to hear from you again. Glad Can Paixano delivered - that place is special. Paris is my favorite city for exactly this kind of dining, so let me give you two options.

OPTION 1: Le Petit Comptoir

What it is: A true neighborhood bistro tucked away on Rue de la Roquette in the 11th. Third-generation chef Michel Durand cooks the same classic French dishes his grandmother made, using ingredients he sources from Marche d'Aligre - same vendor for 30 years. Handwritten chalkboard menu that changes daily. Worn wooden tables, vintage neighborhood photos on the walls, regulars who have "their" table. This is the Paris that existed before Instagram.

What to order:
- Boeuf bourguignon (the signature, made properly)
- Duck confit
- Tarte Tatin for dessert
- House wine from their small Loire producer - genuinely good and priced fairly
- Whatever the daily special is on the chalkboard

Neighborhood: 11th arrondissement, about 10 minutes walk from Place de la Bastille. This is a living, working Parisian neighborhood - not a museum. The 11th has the highest concentration of actual neighborhood bistros left in Paris.

Price: Around 28 euros per person with wine. Remarkable value.

How to book: Phone only (+33 1 43 79 12 47). No website, no online booking. Reservations recommended but walk-ins accepted. Call in French if you can, but they will manage. Note the hours carefully - they are only open for dinner Friday and Saturday. Tuesday through Thursday is lunch only (closes 14:30). Closed Sunday and Monday.

Tourist trap risk: Extremely low. Here is why: only 187 Google reviews and 43 on TripAdvisor (compare that to thousands for tourist spots). Very high local popularity, very low tourist awareness. Menu is in French only. Staff speaks French only. No English menu, no Instagram spots, no website. The pacing is for people who live there, not people catching a flight.

Watch-outs: Small space, not wheelchair accessible, no vegetarian options to speak of, service can seem brusque (this is normal Parisian bistro style, not rudeness). Limited dinner availability - only Friday and Saturday nights.

Conflicts of interest: None. No partnership, no commission, no affiliate relationship.

OPTION 2: Cafe de la Tour

What it is: A classic Parisian brasserie near the Eiffel Tower with terrace seating and direct tower views. They serve traditional French dishes - coq au vin, French onion soup, steak frites, creme brulee.

What to order:
- Coq au vin
- French onion soup
- Steak frites
- Creme brulee
- They offer complimentary sparkling wine for special occasions

Neighborhood: 7th arrondissement, 3 minutes walk from the Eiffel Tower. Classic tourist Paris.

Price: Around 65 euros per person. Significantly more expensive.

How to book: Easy online booking at their website, also accept walk-ins. Very easy to get a table.

Tourist trap risk: I have to be honest with you here, Alex. This is high. Very high tourist popularity, low local popularity. They have menus in 12 languages. Staff speaks 10 languages. Professional photographer available. The space is designed for the Eiffel Tower photo opportunity. Google rating is 4.1 but Yelp drops to 3.5 - that gap often indicates tourist-versus-local perception differences. The quality designation in my files is listed as "bad."

Conflicts of interest: Full transparency - I have a 20 percent commission arrangement with this restaurant. They are a partner of my service.

MY ACTUAL TAKE:

Alex, you told me you want what Can Paixano was for Barcelona - a real local spot, not optimized for visitors, where you can have a proper dinner. Based on that, Cafe de la Tour is not what you are looking for. It is a tourist-oriented spot near a major landmark, and despite my financial incentive to send you there, it does not match what you have asked for.

Le Petit Comptoir is the Can Paixano of this conversation. It is harder to book, less convenient, French-only, no website, limited hours - but it is the real thing. Chef Durand cooking his grandmother's recipes with ingredients from the same market vendor for three decades. That is the kind of place you remember.

The main constraint is timing: dinner is only Friday and Saturday. If those nights work for your schedule, call them. If you need a weekday dinner, let me know and I will dig up another authentic option.

I recommend Le Petit Comptoir.

