Great questions, Alex - these are exactly the practical things you need to know before showing up.

On reservations: No, Can Paixano doesn't take reservations at all. There's no website, no booking system - you just show up. But here's the thing: because it's standing room and people cycle through quickly, you won't be turned away. The average time people spend there is around 30 minutes. You might wait 10 minutes to muscle your way to a good spot at the bar during peak hours, but it's not like a sit-down restaurant where you'd be stuck without a table. My advice would be to either go early, around 6 PM before the after-work rush, or later, after 9 PM when it thins out a bit. If you arrive and it feels overwhelming, you can always grab a drink, get a feel for the flow, and settle in.

On whether it's a full meal: This is a fair concern. The bocadillos are genuinely large - we're talking substantial sandwiches stuffed with high-quality cured meats and cheese. Most locals treat it as a full early dinner or a very generous aperitivo. If you order a couple of bocadillos, some manchego, and a few glasses of cava, you'll leave satisfied. That said, it's not a three-course dining experience. You'll spend maybe 12 euros total.

If you want a longer, more leisurely sit-down dinner, this might not scratch that itch. But for authentic, delicious, and memorably local? It delivers.

Does that address your concerns, or would you like me to think through alternatives?

